{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And in all that I have said, etc.</b> The reason for His saying <b>and in all</b> etc. <b>you shall guard yourselves</b>, and not saying 'and all that' etc. 'you shall guard yourselves' — He intends to say on the path of their saying, of blessed memory (Makkot 23b), that the number of positive commandments is 248, corresponding to the 248 limbs of a person, and 365 negative commandments, corresponding to his 365 sinews (Zohar I 170b). And this is what He alluded to — that a person should not say, 'I will fulfill such and such commandments and that will suffice for my guarding.' Therefore He said <b>and in all that</b> etc., meaning: in all the commandments you must be guarded, and with the lacking of even one of them, the guarding will be lacking for that limb that corresponds to it." "<b>And the name of [other] elohim, etc.</b> He comes to say: just as guarding all the commandments benefits every person for the tally of his limbs and 365 sinews, so too there is a guarding for the totality of a person through the denial of idolatry — for whoever denies idolatry is as though he affirms the entire Torah, and holds fast to a guardian, doing all that his hand finds to do, and all his limbs and sinews will be guarded through the denial of idolatry, even though he did not perform that particular commandment, and the transgression did not come to his hand and he was saved from it. But this is specifically when he is unaware; however, if he blatantly nullifies some of the commandments, he will not be guarded in that limb that corresponds to that commandment which he nullifies, by reason of his denial of idolatry."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:55.726179529Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}